WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.490
You are waiting outside the operating room.

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:09.700
There is nothing left for you to do, yet your mind will not stop, not even for a second.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:20.550
Two thousand years ago Epictetus, a slave in chains, and a thousand years ago al-Ghazali, who walked away from his lectern in Baghdad, searched for the answer to the same question:

4
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.360
why do you still burn over something that is not in your hands?

5
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:40.690
Near present-day Denizli, just above the white travertine terraces of Pamukkale, lies an ancient city: Hierapolis.

6
00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:45.780
Around the middle of the first century AD, a child was born there into slavery.

7
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.310
We do not know his real name.

8
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:58.680
The name history remembers him by is Epictetus, which in Greek means "acquired," or quite simply, "bought." In other words, less a name than a record of ownership.

9
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.450
As a young man, Epictetus was taken to Rome.

10
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:11.420
His master, Epaphroditus, was a powerful man who had risen in the service of the Emperor Nero, and who had once been a slave himself.

11
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.790
Epictetus walked with a limp.

12
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.640
There are two accounts of why.

13
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.770
One says he was lame from birth, or from illness.

14
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:26.820
The other, more famous one, is passed down centuries later by the Christian thinker Origen.

15
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:33.830
According to this story, his master one day clamped Epictetus's leg into an instrument of torture and began to twist it.

16
00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:44.360
Epictetus said, in a calm voice, "You will break it." And when the leg broke, he added, with the same calm: "I told you that you would break it." Is the story true?

17
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.250
We cannot know.

18
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:52.620
But the very fact that such a story was attached to Epictetus tells us what kind of man he was remembered as.

19
00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.550
Even while enslaved, Epictetus was permitted to study philosophy.

20
00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.840
He attended the lectures of the Stoic philosopher Musonius Rufus.

21
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.650
He later won his freedom and began teaching in Rome.

22
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:13.940
When the Emperor Domitian banished the philosophers from Rome, he went to Nicopolis, on the western coast of Greece, and opened a school there.

23
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.990
He never wrote a book.

24
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:23.160
His student Arrian, the same man who would later write the history of Alexander the Great, took notes on his teacher's lectures.

25
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:31.450
Two works came out of those notes: the Discourses, which contain the longer talks, and a small booklet that distills their essence.

26
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.100
Its Greek name is the Enchiridion, meaning the Handbook.

27
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:41.150
And that is where the name comes from: a book to be carried in the hand, to be opened the moment you need it.

28
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.240
The Handbook opens with one of the starkest first lines in the history of philosophy.

29
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.170
According to Epictetus, some things are up to us, and some are not.

30
00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:58.980
What is up to us are our judgments, our impulses, our desires and our aversions; in short, everything that is our own inner business.

31
00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:07.270
What is not up to us are our bodies, our possessions, our reputations, our positions; in short, everything that is not our own business.

32
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:12.680
When we first hear it, this distinction sounds almost too cool-headed to come from a slave.

33
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.930
But when we look at Epictetus's life, we understand why he began with these words.

34
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.260
His body was someone else's property.

35
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.950
Someone else decided where he would go.

36
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:29.520
Even his leg, if the story is true, was at the mercy of another man's whim.

37
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:35.730
Only one realm remained that no one could touch: how he looked at what happened, and what he chose to do.

38
00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:42.100
Epictetus built his entire philosophy on that narrow ground, and he argued that it was not really narrow at all.

39
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.270
Let us bring this into everyday life.

40
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:49.240
You are driving to work in the morning, and the road is jammed.

41
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.770
The traffic is not in your hands.

42
00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.260
Whether you let them know you will be late for the meeting is.

43
00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:01.150
Whether you pound the steering wheel, or spend that half hour listening to something, is in your hands too.

44
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:06.720
According to Epictetus, most human suffering comes from confusing these two lists.

45
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.610
We treat the traffic as our own business, and we treat our anger as something that simply happens to us.

46
00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.620
In fact, the opposite is true.

47
00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:25.030
One of Epictetus's most often quoted lines says exactly this: it is not things themselves that disturb people, but their judgments about those things.

48
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:30.760
Death is not terrible, he says; what is terrible is the judgment that death is terrible.

49
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.250
This should not be taken as a denial of real pain.

50
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.540
Elsewhere, Epictetus speaks of a ship setting out on a voyage.

51
00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.190
You choose the captain, the crew, the day.

52
00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:45.680
But if a storm rises, your part is over.

53
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:52.050
If the ship is sinking, the only thing left for you to do is to go down not in terror, but without fear.

54
00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.940
It sounds beautiful.

55
00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:03.030
The trouble is that reading this in daylight over a cup of tea is one thing; putting it into practice at three in the morning while staring at the ceiling is quite another.

56
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.040
It is three twelve in the morning.

57
00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.530
Your phone screen lights up for a moment.

58
00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:13.020
No notification, just the time.

59
00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.350
Tomorrow morning a test result is coming back.

60
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:20.480
Or the answer from a job interview will arrive.

61
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:28.930
Or your child set off on the road at midnight and still has not texted to say "I made it." You toss and turn in bed.

62
00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.780
You know that thinking will not change a thing.

63
00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.270
The test result is already settled in the lab.

64
00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.560
The interview decision may have been made long ago.

65
00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.570
The road is the same road whether you sleep or not.

66
00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:46.380
And still, the mind will not let go.

67
00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:51.150
One scenario, then another, then a worse version of the first.

68
00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:55.760
Why is knowing Epictetus's distinction not enough on nights like these?

69
00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:59.410
Why can we not let go of something we cannot control?

70
00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:03.060
Psychology looks at this question from several angles.

71
00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.590
One of them is the illusion of control.

72
00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:10.840
In nineteen seventy-five, the psychologist Ellen Langer published an experiment.

73
00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:15.130
She sold lottery tickets to people working in an office.

74
00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:19.900
Some were allowed to choose their own ticket; others simply had one handed to them.

75
00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:23.870
As the drawing approached, she asked to buy the tickets back.

76
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:31.040
Those who had chosen their own ticket asked, on average, for more than four times as much money as those who had been handed one.

77
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.370
Yet the odds of winning were exactly the same.

78
00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:40.420
The mere act of choosing created a feeling that we might, even slightly, influence the outcome.

79
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.910
Midnight anxiety works a little like this.

80
00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:49.000
The mind believes that thinking about something endlessly is a way of keeping it under control.

81
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.290
As if, by letting go of the worry, we would be abandoning our post.

82
00:06:53.290 --> 00:07:00.140
As if, should something bad happen, being able to say "but I thought it through enough" would somehow protect us.

83
00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:03.790
Here, anxiety turns into a kind of magic.

84
00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:08.720
We stay awake like someone standing guard, yet there is nothing being guarded.

85
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.850
The second angle is intolerance of uncertainty.

86
00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:20.820
Researchers who study anxiety disorders observe that some people are troubled less by a bad outcome than by not knowing what the outcome will be.

87
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:26.870
Sometimes a person actually feels relief on hearing bad news, because now they can do something.

88
00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.680
In uncertainty, there is nothing to hold on to.

89
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.010
The mind fills that void with scenarios.

90
00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.140
And building scenarios feels like doing something.

91
00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:41.310
The third angle is older, and more human.

92
00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:45.440
The things we are asked to let go of are very often the things we love.

93
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.370
On Epictetus's list of what is not up to us are our body and our possessions.

94
00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:58.340
But the things that keep us awake at night are usually other people: a sick mother, an unemployed spouse, a child out on the road.

95
00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.950
Setting them aside as "not up to us" feels like a lack of love.

96
00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:07.400
Anxiety is held on to as a kind of proof of love.

97
00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:12.970
Epictetus addressed this objection too, and his answer still sounds harsh today.

98
00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:19.660
In the Handbook, he says that when you kiss your child, you should remind yourself that you are kissing a mortal.

99
00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:28.110
If a jug you are fond of breaks, you are not distressed, because you knew it was a jug; carry the same awareness for the people you love.

100
00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.760
On first reading, it seems almost cruel.

101
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:39.250
But what Epictetus is saying is not "do not love." What he is saying is this: know that what you love does not belong to you.

102
00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:42.900
It has been entrusted to you; you are not its owner.

103
00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:49.110
In his view, when we lose something, we should learn to say not "I have lost it," but "I have given it back."

104
00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.400
Many people find this answer too cold, and rightly so.

105
00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:59.450
Telling someone sitting on the edge of the bed at three twelve in the morning to "remember the jug" is not much help.

106
00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:04.380
This is where Stoicism's strength lies, but it is also where its limit lies.

107
00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:09.150
It draws the distinction very sharply, yet it leaves one thing open.

108
00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:14.720
How do I keep doing the part that is in my hands, and to what do I surrender the part that is not?

109
00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.330
To the void, to fate, to the rational order of the universe?

110
00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.820
The Stoics gave their own answer to this question.

111
00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:29.190
For them, the universe was a whole governed by reason, by the logos, and everything happened as it was meant to.

112
00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:36.840
But the same question was also asked in another civilization, in another language, in the middle of the desert, beside a camel.

113
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:45.610
And there, the answer travelled from tongue to tongue for centuries, because it resolved the tension between action and letting go in a single sentence.

114
00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:50.060
In the hadith collection of Imam al-Tirmidhi, there is a brief scene.

115
00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:59.150
A Bedouin comes to the Prophet with his camel and asks: Should I tie up my camel and then put my trust in God, or should I let it loose and put my trust in God?

116
00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:03.440
The answer is short: "Tie it, and put your trust in God."

117
00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:12.690
In Turkish, this saying settled into the form "Tie your camel, then trust in God." Its power comes from making the question itself seem misplaced.

118
00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:20.660
The Bedouin believes he has to choose between two options: either he relies on his own precautions, or he relies on God.

119
00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:23.670
The answer tells him that these are not rivals.

120
00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:27.960
Tying the rope is the work of action; trusting is the work of the heart.

121
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.770
Neither one takes the place of the other.

122
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:39.580
Even so, the concept of tawakkul, of trust in God, was always debated throughout the history of Islamic thought.

123
00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:47.390
In some Sufi circles, especially in the early centuries, there were those who understood tawakkul as abandoning every kind of precaution.

124
00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:56.960
There are stories of people who set out into the desert without provisions, who refused medicine when they fell ill, who considered pursuing a livelihood a sign of weak faith.

125
00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:00.450
Against this, a strong objection arose.

126
00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:10.980
A saying attributed to Sahl al-Tustari sums it up well: whoever condemns working to earn a living has condemned the Prophet's way, and whoever condemns trust in God has condemned faith itself.

127
00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:16.870
In other words, the two belong to separate layers, and one does not cancel the other.

128
00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:21.800
One of the figures who treated this debate most systematically was al-Ghazali.

129
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:32.650
At the end of the eleventh century, he gave up his brilliant teaching career in Baghdad and set out on a long search, and in his great work, the Revival of the Religious Sciences,

130
00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.660
he devotes an entire section to tawakkul.

131
00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.190
His tone is almost mocking.

132
00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:48.560
Someone who sits with food in front of him, does not reach out his hand, and waits for God to feed him, he says, has not attained trust in God; he has lost his mind.

133
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:54.130
The same goes for someone who expects a harvest without sowing seed, or a child without marrying.

134
00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:59.540
For al-Ghazali, abandoning the means is not trust in God; it is ignorance.

135
00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:06.070
But clinging to the means and mistaking them for the true author of the outcome is another kind of error.

136
00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:11.960
You sow the seed, but you do not make the rain fall, and you do not bring the soil to life.

137
00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:17.210
Tawakkul is doing the work of your hands while binding your heart to the true author of the outcome.

138
00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:21.180
Al-Ghazali also divides tawakkul into degrees.

139
00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:26.750
At the lowest degree, a person is like a client who trusts a good representative.

140
00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:32.480
He has handed his case to a lawyer he trusts, and because of that, he is at ease.

141
00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:37.090
At a higher degree, he is like a child in his mother's arms.

142
00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:42.500
The child does not even think that his mother will protect him; he simply takes refuge in her.

143
00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:49.030
The point al-Ghazali draws our attention to is this: in both examples, the person is doing something.

144
00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:53.800
The client brings his case, the child runs to his mother.

145
00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:56.490
Tawakkul is not inaction.

146
00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.660
It is about what your action leans on.

147
00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:06.510
A little more than a century later, in Konya, Rumi asks the same question, this time through a story.

148
00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:13.360
In the first book of the Masnavi, the animals of a forest have grown weary of a lion that attacks them every day.

149
00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:19.570
They bring the lion an offer: do not go out hunting, and every day we will send you one of our own.

150
00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:23.060
Leave your sustenance to God, and trust in Him.

151
00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:27.350
The lion objects, and a long debate begins between them.

152
00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:31.960
The animals argue for trust; the lion argues for effort.

153
00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:40.410
Through the lion's voice, Rumi refers to the famous hadith: the Prophet said aloud, trust in God, and tie your camel's knee.

154
00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:44.060
The one who earns his living is the beloved of God.

155
00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:48.030
If you trust in God, trust in Him in your work as well.

156
00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:56.160
The interesting thing about the story is this: in the end, the animals win the debate, yet Rumi does not dismiss what the lion said.

157
00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:02.370
In the Masnavi's view, to work is to refuse to deny the hands and feet that God has given you.

158
00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:06.180
If you have been given an oar, that oar is a sign.

159
00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:11.270
But the real point is not to imagine, while you row, that you own the sea.

160
00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:15.880
Here we can return to Epictetus and set the two ideas side by side.

161
00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:23.530
The slave from Hierapolis and the scholar from Tus draw the same line: what is your business, and what is not.

162
00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.980
Both ask you to do what is your business completely.

163
00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:31.630
Both tell you that the outcome does not belong to you.

164
00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.960
The difference lies at the other end of the rope.

165
00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:40.050
For Epictetus, at the other end there is an impersonal universe ordered by reason.

166
00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:43.860
To him, letting go is a wise acceptance.

167
00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:50.710
For al-Ghazali and Rumi, at the other end there is a Lord who is trusted, who loves, who sees.

168
00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:55.480
To them, letting go is surrender, but what you surrender to is not a void.

169
00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:58.330
It is Someone who hears you.

170
00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:02.620
At three twelve in the morning, this difference becomes something important.

171
00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:16.510
Where the Stoic can only say, "this is not in my hands," the person who practices tawakkul can take one step further: "It is not in my hands, but it is not left to chance either." The folk saying "the planning is ours,

172
00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:20.320
the decree is God's" puts it simply.

173
00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:23.490
For a believer, this is consolation.

174
00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:30.500
And even for a non-believer, the idea that anxiety can be set down somewhere can, on its own, open a door.

175
00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:36.390
This true meaning of tawakkul also offers a quiet answer to those who confuse it with laziness.

176
00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:49.640
The lazy person does not tie the rope, and when the camel is lost, he says, "It was fate." The anxious person ties the rope, then keeps watch beside it all night, then ties it again, then brings yet another rope.

177
00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.250
The person who practices tawakkul ties the rope firmly and goes to sleep.

178
00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:57.100
All three are looking at the same camel.

179
00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.750
What separates them is where they let go of the end of the rope.

180
00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.520
So we tied the rope, we did all we could, and the camel ran away anyway.

181
00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.570
What then?

182
00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:11.540
There are few people who do not know the weight of this question.

183
00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:20.630
A relative of someone who is ill does everything the doctors say, has every test done on time, gives every medicine on the hour, and still loses them.

184
00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:24.760
A student studies for a whole year and falls ill on the day of the exam.

185
00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:30.330
A shopkeeper puts all his savings into his shop, and a crisis wipes everything out.

186
00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:36.060
In such moments, the sentence "I did all I could" can be said in two ways.

187
00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:40.350
One with rebellion: I did it, but what good did it do?

188
00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:44.800
The other with a kind of peace: I did it, and the rest was never mine.

189
00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.570
To describe this second attitude, the Stoics use a beautiful image.

190
00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:54.820
Cicero, in a work explaining Stoic ethics, gives the example of an archer.

191
00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:58.470
The archer does everything in his power to hit the target.

192
00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:04.040
He chooses his bow well, takes aim, reckons the wind, and releases the arrow.

193
00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:09.450
But once the arrow has left the bow, a gust may blow, or the target may shift.

194
00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:15.660
For the Stoics, the archer's true goal is not to strike the board, but to shoot well.

195
00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:20.590
Hitting the board is something preferred, but what is truly good is the shot itself.

196
00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:24.560
We find a similar idea at another far end of the East.

197
00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:36.850
In the Bhagavad Gita, one of the foundational texts of Indian thought, the god Krishna says to the warrior Arjuna, who hesitates on the battlefield: your right is to action alone, never to its fruits.

198
00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.460
Do not act for the sake of the fruit, and do not fall into inaction either.

199
00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:50.230
Three separate traditions, three separate languages, arriving at the same place: the effort belongs to you, the outcome does not.

200
00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:58.200
If we look at how this idea finds its echo in lives today, perhaps the most striking example is the American pilot James Stockdale.

201
00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:04.090
In September of nineteen sixty-five, his plane was shot down over North Vietnam.

202
00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:12.380
As he drifted down on his parachute, by his own account, he felt he was leaving the world of technology and entering the world of Epictetus.

203
00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:17.470
He carried the Handbook he had read in his university years with him, in his memory.

204
00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:21.440
For roughly the next seven and a half years he remained a prisoner.

205
00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.410
He was tortured and held alone in a cell for long stretches.

206
00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:31.780
In his later writings, he explained that what kept him standing was that first distinction of Epictetus.

207
00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:35.910
He could not change the cell, the pain, or the guard.

208
00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:44.040
But his own honor, his loyalty to his fellow prisoners, what he would say and what he would refuse to say, those he still chose for himself.

209
00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:51.850
The words of an enslaved philosopher, spoken some nineteen hundred years earlier, had become the branch a man clung to in a prison camp.

210
00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:56.460
The same idea quietly found its way into modern psychology as well.

211
00:18:56.460 --> 00:19:09.230
In the middle of the twentieth century, Albert Ellis, one of the pioneers of cognitive therapy, openly credited Epictetus when describing the roots of his approach, citing the line: "People are disturbed not by things,

212
00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:21.520
but by their judgments about things." Today, when a therapist asks, "Is this thought true, or is it your interpretation?", you can hear the echo of that small school in Nicopolis.

213
00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:33.170
The short prayer attributed to the American theologian Reinhold Niebuhr, recited in support groups in many parts of the world, asks for the very same thing: the serenity to accept what I cannot change,

214
00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:37.940
the courage to change what I can, and the wisdom to know the difference.

215
00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:46.390
But perhaps the deepest legacy of this idea lies hidden not in books or in therapy rooms, but in an ordinary moment of farewell.

216
00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:53.560
Islamic thought calls the mature form of tawakkul "rida." Rida does not mean loving the outcome.

217
00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:56.890
No one is content to lose someone they love.

218
00:19:56.890 --> 00:20:00.060
Rida means ceasing to fight with what has happened.

219
00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:05.950
It means letting the wound remain where it is, while refusing to let it take over the whole of one's life.

220
00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:10.240
Let us recall al-Ghazali's image of the child in its mother's arms.

221
00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:13.250
When the child falls, it cries.

222
00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:16.100
Crying does not break trust.

223
00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:19.750
Even as it cries, it clings to its mother.

224
00:20:19.750 --> 00:20:24.840
The peace carried in the sentence "I did everything I could" comes from here as well.

225
00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.170
This sentence is not a declaration of indifference.

226
00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:30.380
It is the settling of an account.

227
00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.390
It says: I have paid my share.

228
00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:38.800
I tied the rope, I loosed the arrow, I had the tests done, I knocked on the door.

229
00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:42.450
Carrying the outcome was never the work of my shoulders.

230
00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:47.220
Human shoulders were not made to carry outcomes; they can carry only effort.

231
00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:54.710
When we try to shoulder the outcome as well, we become that weary person staring at the ceiling at twelve minutes past three in the morning.

232
00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:59.000
Epictetus taught in Nicopolis until the end of his life.

233
00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:05.050
According to tradition, he owned nothing in his house but a straw mat, a bed, and a lamp.

234
00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.900
One day his iron lamp was stolen.

235
00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:15.390
The next day he bought a clay lamp in its place, and he is said to have remarked: the thief could take from me no more than a lamp's worth.

236
00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:19.200
It could be stolen again tomorrow, and perhaps it will be.

237
00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:23.970
But Epictetus did not give up on the lamp, and he did not choose to sit in the dark.

238
00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:29.220
He bought a new lamp, lit it, and went on with his teaching.

239
00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:32.390
Perhaps the whole matter is this simple.

240
00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:36.680
To tie the camel, to light the lamp, to loose the arrow.

241
00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:43.050
And then to leave the end of the rope, the fate of the light, and the path of the arrow to something greater than oneself.

242
00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:45.420
Letting go is not losing.

243
00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:50.670
Sometimes the only freedom left in a person's hands is the ability to open them.

244
00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:54.640
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Felsefe.

245
00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.490
Until we meet again in a new story.
